DOCUMENT();

############################################################
# Load Macros
############################################################
loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "PGML.pl",
  "AnswerFormatHelp.pl",
  "contextForm.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl",
);
COMMENT('Authored in PreTeXt');

############################################################
# Header
############################################################
TEXT(beginproblem());

############################################################
# PG Setup Code
############################################################
    Context("Numeric");
$a = random(1,4);
$b = random(5,10);
$c = random(1,10);
Context()->variables->are(x=>"Real",y=>"Real");
$constraint = Formula("$a*x^2+$b*y^2")->reduce();
$LM1 = Compute(2*$a);
$LM2 = Compute(2*$b);
$ans = Compute("(2*$c)/(($a*$b)^(1/2))")->reduce();

############################################################
# Body
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML
Find the maximum area of a rectangle which is inscribed in [`[$constraint]=[$c]\text{.}`]

END_PGML

############################################################
# Solution
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
The area of the rectangle is [`f(x,y)=4xy`] and the constraint is [`g(x,y)=[$constraint]-[$c]=0\text{.}`] Due to the Lagrange Multiplier method, we have [`\vec{\nabla f}=\lambda \vec{\nabla g}\text{,}`] which gives the following vector equation:

[```\langle 4y, 4x\rangle=\lambda \langle [$LM1] x, [$LM2] y\rangle\text{.}```]

This vector equation is equivalent to these two scalar equations:

[```4y=[$LM1]\lambda x\text{,}```]

[```4x=[$LM2]\lambda y\text{.}```]

We divide the above equations:

[```\newcommand{\R}{\mathbb{R}}\frac{4y}{4x}=\frac{[$LM1] \lambda x}{[$LM2] \lambda y} 
\Rightarrow \frac{y}{x}=\frac{[$LM1] x}{[$LM2] y} \Rightarrow y^2=\frac{[$a]}{[$b]} x^2\text{.}```]

Then, we replace this equation into [`g(x,y)=[$constraint]-[$c]=0`] to get [`[$a]x^2+[$a]x^2=[$c]\text{.}`] Hence, [`x=\sqrt{\frac{[$c]}{[$LM1]}}`] and [`y=\sqrt{\frac{[$c]}{[$LM2]}}`] and the maximum area is [`4\left(\sqrt{\frac{[$c]}{[$LM1]}}\right)\left(\sqrt{\frac{[$c]}{[$LM2]}}\right)=[$ans]\text{.}`]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

############################################################
# End Problem
